#{[17592186522803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522803 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522803 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522803 #edb/ref 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1ac1f29-7051-5497-b0bb-1e7a6a92e87b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522803 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522808 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522808 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522808 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522808 #edb/ref 1071 "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522808 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7127aac-4823-5d27-b379-06474be0fe41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522808 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522823 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c438b31-61c8-58dc-acc5-f28ab811272c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522853 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b36ab65-7aff-5752-af77-f619ccede402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522855 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80749284-e241-5549-b174-88307b7b6777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522855 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e15523fe-4f49-557d-ba8f-2d82dda09940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186522782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186522853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921865